{
  "requirement": "Bedrock configurations MAY supply `credentials` containing nonempty\n`access_key_id`, `secret_access_key`, and optional nonempty `session_token`.\nOllama configurations MAY supply a nonempty `api_key`. Habeas MUST reject a\nBedrock record combining explicit credentials with `aws_profile`, and an Ollama\nrecord combining `api_key` with `api_key_env`.\n\nExplicit credentials MUST be passed directly to the selected provider without\nchanging process environment variables or writing credential files. Saved\nexplicit values MUST round-trip; credentials resolved from profiles or the\nenvironment MUST NOT be serialized back. Debug output, configuration diagnostics,\nusage records, and initial prompts MUST NOT include credential values. TOML\ndiagnostics MUST NOT echo credential-containing source lines. An active workspace\nfile containing explicit credentials MUST be excluded from model source reads by\ndefault under {cite}`requirements.authoring.source_tools`; explicit workspace path\noverrides still apply.\n\nDocumentation MUST discourage literal credentials in workspace files and\nrecommend AWS profiles or environment-variable references. Examples MUST use\nplaceholders and explain that workspace files may be committed or read as source\nwhen explicitly allowed.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge that the `{impl}` code and tests satisfy every obligation in `requirements.authoring.credentials`. I found no unmarked implementing code and no acknowledged FIXME gaps.\n\n**Validation and rejection** (`src/config.rs`, `BackendConfig::validate`)\n- Bedrock `credentials.access_key_id` and `secret_access_key` must be nonempty. `session_token` is optional but must be nonempty if present.\n- Ollama `api_key` must be nonempty.\n- `credentials` together with `aws_profile` is rejected, and so is `api_key` together with `api_key_env`.\n- `tests/configuration.rs::explicit_credentials_round_trip_but_diagnostics_and_debug_redact_them` covers the profile and `api_key_env` conflicts and the empty `secret_access_key`, `session_token` and `api_key` cases. The empty `access_key_id` case is not tested by name, but it runs through the same loop in `validate` as the other two fields.\n\n**Direct passing to the provider** (`src/authoring/providers.rs`)\n- `bedrock_sdk_loader` calls `credentials_provider(Credentials::new(..))` on the SDK loader. It sets no environment variables and writes no files.\n- `RigProvider::connect` calls `provider.with_api_key(key)` for Ollama.\n- The test `explicit_bedrock_credentials_reach_the_sdk_without_the_default_chain` checks the key, secret, session token and region on the resulting SDK config.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs::authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file` checks that the literal Ollama key arrives in the request headers.\n- No test asserts the absence of environment mutation or credential files. The implementation is a single provider-builder call, so I consider that absence acceptable.\n\n**Round-trip and non-serialization**\n- `SecretString` derives `Serialize` transparently, so saved values round-trip. That is covered by `configuration_structure` and `configuration_serialization`, whose text states this, and the test asserts `parse(to_toml(config)) == config`.\n- Environment-resolved keys come from `OllamaConfig::api_key_from` as a separate `String`. The test `ollama_authentication_resolves_separately_and_rejects_missing_values` asserts the serialized TOML contains `MODEL_KEY` but not `private-value`.\n\n**Redaction**\n- `Debug` for `SecretString` prints `<redacted>`, and the round-trip test asserts no secret appears in the config's `Debug` output.\n- `decode` strips the TOML input from errors and replaces any value found under `credentials` or `api_key` with `<redacted>`. `parse_document` reports only the line and column of a syntax error, never the source line.\n- The tests cover wrong-typed values, a trailing-garbage line and an integer key. They confirm that neither the error text nor the evidence-config path leaks a secret.\n- `authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file` asserts the key is absent from the request body (so not in the initial prompt), the usage record, stdout and stderr.\n\n**Workspace file exclusion**\n- `load_workspace_evidence` sets `contains_credentials` when any `authoring.configs.*` table has `credentials` or `api_key`.\n- `PathPolicy::contains_credentials` and `SourceAccess::path` deny the file, including via symlink or canonical aliases.\n- Every tool goes through `path()` or `file()`: `habeas`, `outline`, `list_files` (entries are filtered out) and `ripgrep` (per-file check while walking).\n- An explicit allow is honoured because `allowed()` is checked before the credential check.\n- `workspace_credentials_are_excluded_unless_explicitly_allowed` tests both the denial, with no secret in the message, and the allow override.\n- `evidence_configuration_ignores_unused_authoring_settings_and_detects_credentials` tests detection.\n- The tool descriptions in `src/authoring/tools.rs` state the exclusion.\n\n**Documentation** (`md/requirements/authoring.md`, \"Editing the file\", and `md/reference.md`)\n- Both prefer profiles and environment references.\n- Both say literal credentials are \"not recommended\", \"especially in a workspace file that might be committed\".\n- The literal-credential example uses uppercase placeholders and says to keep the file out of version control.\n- Both say an explicit allow exposes the file to the model and records it as evidence.\n- Both warn against combining `credentials` with `aws_profile` and `api_key` with `api_key_env`.",
